00:00:00.359 --> 00:00:02.090 align:start position:0%
 
six months since the start of the  Ukraine war and the parallels with  Taiwan remain in Focus large  authoritarian Powers with global  Ambitions claiming Sovereign rights over  a smaller Democratic neighbor the  Kremlin has steadfastly dismissed  Ukraine's right to exist as a nation  insisting that it historically belongs  to Russia likewise China sees the island  of Taiwan as an indivisible part of the  Homeland and has promised a military  response should it ever declare  independence  Taiwan fears that it could be next to  suffer an invasion are clearly not  unjustified but six months after Russia  crossed the border into Ukraine East  China any closer to doing the same in  Taiwan recent months have seen  attentions across the Taiwan Strait  climbed to the highest level in years  spurred on by high-ranking visits from  the United States House speaker Nancy  Pelosi's trip to Taipei just a few weeks  ago Drew an angry response from Beijing  which launched massive military drills  around Taiwan  the parallels haven't been lost on  Russia's president who'd loudly supports  China's territorial claim to Taiwan and  accuses the U.S of provocation  Americans the American Adventure in  relation to Taiwan is not just a trick  by an individual irresponsible  politician but part of a purposeful  conscious U.S strategy to destabilize  and make chaotic the situation in the  region and the world  for more on this let's bring in DW's  William young in our Taipei Bureau  William what is the feeling over there  has the Ukraine war signaled a new phase  in China's aggression towards Taiwan  I think there's definitely an added  feeling of urgency over here in Taipei  since February especially among the  citizens we have already seen an  increase of people participating in  self-organized uh training for self-help  and first aid uh kind of activities and  at the same time there are also  companies private companies that are  offering weapons boot camps for those  who are hoping to acquire more combat  Readiness experience and at the same  time people are also expressing their  willingness to be participating in the  government's renewed kind of increased  intensity for the reservist training  schemes so I think these are all the  signs that compared to the past when  Tony's people felt like the military  threat from China is still kind of like  a distant thing in the future the  Ukraine war made them realize that  rather than hoping that other countries  will stepping and intervene when China  prepares to militarily invade Taiwan  they Taiwanese people should also have  the ability to defend itself because at  the end of the day they think and they  see what happened to Ukraine is that  most of the actual fighting is coming  from the Taiwanese people and the  Taiwanese troops you know you talked  about international support let's talk  about that Ukraine has had a string of  high-level visits from U.S officials as  the Ukraine war strengthened  International support for Taiwan  yes definitely I think we saw a couple  different delegations visiting Taiwan  including the high very controversial  visit of the U.S House speaker Pelosi  and even though that visit really  triggered a heightened response from  China including a seven-day military  exercise around Taiwan but we are  continuing to see different delegations  including from the US and currently  there is a Japanese parliamentary  delegation and later this year there is  going to be a UK Parliament delegation a  Canadian Parliament delegation and the  Danish Parliament delegation all  scheduled to visit Taiwan in the coming  months I think these are all signs that  despite beijing's warning and these very  provocative and heightened and more  dangerous kind of military exercise and  activities around Taiwan it is not  deterring the International Community to  show its support by sending their  delegations to visit Taiwan thank you so  much for joining us William young DW  reporter in Taipei  so while China has set its sights on  Taiwan it has firmly refused to condemn  Russia for its war in Ukraine something  born out of the No Limits friendship  Chinese president Xi Jinping and Russian  President Vladimir Putin agreed to at  the Winter Olympics in Beijing in early  February and since then China has sought  to frame Russia's actions in Ukraine as  being the only option Moscow had to push  back against U.S led expansionism  the key to solving the Ukraine crisis  lies in the hands of the United States  and NATO we hope the United States and  NATO as the culprits of the crisis can  reflect on their roles in the Ukraine  crisis take up their due  responsibilities and make practical  actions to resolve the conflict  and for more on the Chinese thinking  here let's bring in Reed Standish he's a  correspondent with radio free Europe  covering China's influence across  Eastern Europe and Central Asia and he  joins me now from Prague Reed welcome  does China see an opportunity with the  Ukraine war to dislodge the United  States from its position of influence in  Asia  um well I there's no doubt that the  Ukraine war has changed things I think  geopolitically everywhere but I think  especially in Asia and especially in  regards to to Taiwan obviously Taiwan  has presented itself as a bit of a foil  um for everything that's been happening  in Ukraine over the last six months so I  think for Taiwan if anything I don't  know if it's seeing it as an opportunity  to necessarily dislodge the United  States from Asia but I think it's quite  worried that this is perhaps one of the  last Windows of opportunity to limit  American influence and especially  American attempts to reorient its  military presence and a lot of its  foreign policy making towards the  Pacific away from Europe This invasion  in effect has forced the United States  to turn its attention towards Europe at  a time when American leaders have been  talking about uh China and the Pacific  being the main theater of The Next  Century so I think China sees the  Ukraine war as this this factor that has  really changed things and I think that  they know know that they have a short  window and if they're going to secure  their interests and prevent America from  from going against theirs  right let's speak about the Chinese  influence in the Pacific and uh in the  in in wider Asia does the No Limits  partnership it has with Russia give it  an upper hand in achieving that goal  well it's  it's declared this no limits partnership  in Beijing  um at the launch of the Winter Olympics  um it's clear that policy Bankers in  Beijing they they are invested in this  relationship and given everything that  we've seen Russia do whether that's been  you know war crimes everything all the  the violence and violations we've seen  on the ground in Ukraine none of that  has really seemed to have damaged this  partnership and the main reason for that  is that Beijing knows that it needs  um a superpower like Russia one with  especially its Security Council seat and  its nuclear power or its nuclear weapons  and its influence around the world on  its side if it's going to uh grow its  influence and especially if it's going  to resist what it sees as U.S enrochment  on its interests right you spoke about  Taiwan as well let's just talk a bit  about that now there's a lot of views  that when it comes to the lessons that  China is drawing from the Ukraine war  but very specifically Reid is it is is  China watching the Western response to  Russia as an indicator of what could be  in store for it if it invaded Taiwan  indicator of how the West had react  Taipei has also been watching very  closely  um uh learning military lessons from  Ukraine on the ground about how to  resist the potential Invasion but going  back to your original question  um I I think definitely there's been a  lot of uh especially in regards to  sanctions I think that the the economic  response especially from the European  Union and the United States and the West  at large and how unified it has been and  how deep cutting it has been I think not  only caught Moscow off guard but also  cut Beijing off guard and obviously the  Chinese economy is much larger than the  Russian economy it's much more  integrated globally and the West is much  more dependent on it than say Russia is  um so I think that maybe Beijing feels  it has a little bit more cover here in  terms of getting hit by sanctions but  seeing that kind of response I think has  as we're in China have they been looking  into and exploring different ways about  how to protect themselves especially  against uh sanctions which could  obviously hurt their economy which is uh  the sen natural factor of the Communist  party's hold on on power in China  Reid we lived there for the time being  but thank you so much for joining us  today read Standish from Radio free  Europe thank you so much  like China India too refused to condemn  Russia for its Invasion but unlike China  it wasn't an act of support for Moscow  but a strategic choice for New Delhi the  two countries have very strong ties  dating back to the Cold War and Russia  still supplies the bulk of India's  weapons it's a time-tested friendship  that saw the foreign Ministers of both  sides meet in Delhi in early April with  Russia only too Keen to offer India  whatever it needed  uh we will be ready to supply to India  any uh Goods which India wants to buy  and I have no doubt that a way would be  out to uh  bypass the artificial impediments which  illegal Universal sanctions by the West  created  and we saw how despite sanctions India  increased its purchases of discounted  Russian oil by a factor of five when  compared to last year so does that  provide an indication of where Indian  foreign policy is now headed DW's Delhi  bureau chief Amrita Chima has more  India appears to have found its balance  on the Diplomatic tightrope when it  comes to the war in Ukraine it's no  longer feeling diffident or defensive  about refusing to condemn Russia's  invasion of Ukraine there's now talk  about balancing India's interests and  India's right to buy cheap Russian oil  if it wants now recently the Ukrainian  foreign minister said that India must be  mindful that every barrel of oil that  comes from Russia contains Ukrainian  blood now his counterpart the Indian  foreign minister SJ Shankar said he has  a moral duty to look after the needs of  his people  so in this diplomatic churn you see  India's feeling more confident it wants  to enhance its diplomatic space on the  international stage it wants to use  leverage for its own strategic interests  especially when it comes to countries  like China  the two neighbors are Rivals and have a  very tense relationship now in this  situation if Russia and China come  closer together things could get very  difficult for India because after all  India is very reliant on Russia in many  ways specially military hardware  so India might aspire to have a neutral  position in a multi-polar world but if  things go the way they might go with  Russia and China coming closer together  it would not be easy for India at all

